<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ohio River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> River is a major river of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, flowing about 1,580 km from Pittsburgh to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. In the 18th century the Ohio country was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Native nations, and after 1783 the river was the line that the Western Confederacy demanded as the limit of American settlement. It later divided free and slave states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ohio-river.jpg" alt="Ohio River" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ohiorivermap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Ohio River is 981 miles (1,579 km) long. It begins at the confluence of the Allegheny and Monongahela rivers at what is now Point State Park in Pittsburgh and flows south-westwards to its mouth on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, serving as the boundary between the Midwestern and Southern United States. It is the largest tributary by volume of the Mississippi. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> word Ohi:yo', meaning good river. The Falls of the Ohio below Louisville, a drop of 26 feet in two miles, long obstructed larger commercial navigation until the Louisville and Portland Canal bypassed the rapids in 1830.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For thousands of years Native Americans used the river as a major transport and trading route. In the five centuries before European colonisation the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture</a> built regional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> and earthwork mounds in the valley, among them the Angel Mounds near Evansville. The Osage, Omaha, Ponca and Kaw lived in the Ohio Valley and, under pressure over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> from the Haudenosaunee, migrated west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in the 17th century. Arnout Viele, sent by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1692 to trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, is credited as the first European to explore western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and the upper Ohio Valley.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a> the river divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s trans-Appalachian Indian Reserve into colonial lands to the south and Native American lands to the north. In the late 18th century it became the southern boundary of the Northwest Territory, and it was later part of the border between free and slave territory. Where the river was narrow, thousands of enslaved people escaped across it to the North, many helped by free Black and white people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. The Silver Bridge at Point Pleasant collapsed into the river on 15 December 1967, killing 46 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is extensively industrialised and populated, and regular barge traffic carries oil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and other goods. It is maintained by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers as part of the Mississippi River System.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ohio River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Portsmouth Earthworks, at the meeting of the Scioto and Ohio rivers, combined circular enclosures, two large horseshoe-shaped enclosures and three sets of parallel-walled roads, one of which crossed the <b>Ohio River</b> into Kentucky.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Using biblical references, fugitives called Canada the Promised Land or Heaven and the <b>Ohio River</b>, the boundary between slave and free states, the River Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/movement/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Indiana, lying between Lake Michigan to the northwest and the <b>Ohio River</b> to the south, was admitted to the Union as the 19th state of the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Confederation Congress set out how lands north-west of the <b>Ohio River</b> would be governed and admitted as new states.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Paris of 1783 gave the United States land north of the <b>Ohio River</b> that its Native inhabitants had never ceded.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-confederacy">Western Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
